<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Dal Fiatach</h1><p class="st-meta">450 CE – 1177 CE (approx.) · capital Downpatrick</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ruling dynasty of east County Down, centred on Downpatrick, which claimed to be the true Ulaid and held the Ulster kingship until 1177.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Dál Fiatach were a dynasty of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ulaid">Ulaid</a> in what is now east County Down. They claimed descent from the ancient Ulaid royal line, and after the Ulaid were pushed east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-bann">River Bann</a> they became one of the two main powers in the Kingdom of Ulaid, with their stronghold at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/downpatrick">Dún Lethglaise</a>, Downpatrick, associated with St Patrick's burial.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Dál Fiatach kings alternated the over-kingship of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ulaid">Ulaid</a> with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dal-naraidi">Dal nAraidi</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baetan-mac-cairill">Báetán mac Cairill</a>, who died in 581, is said to have claimed authority as far as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/isle-of-man">Isle of Man</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiachnae-mac-demmain">Fiachnae mac Demmáin</a> fought for the kingship in the early 7th century. Their wealth lay in the fertile lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lecale">Lecale</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ards-peninsula">Ards</a>, and the monasteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangor-abbey">Bangor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movilla-abbey">Movilla</a> lay nearby.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 11th century their kings were the Mac Duinn Sléibe, MacDunleavy. In 1177 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Norman</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-de-courcy">John de Courcy</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/downpatrick">Downpatrick</a> and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruaidri-mac-duinn-sleibe">Ruaidrí Mac Duinn Sléibe</a>, establishing the Anglo-Norman lordship of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-ireland">Ulster</a>, later an earldom, and ending Dál Fiatach rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dal-fiatach.jpg" alt="Dal Fiatach" width="320" height="260"><figcaption>Image: Mabuska · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UlsterDioceseKingdoms.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>450 CE – 1177 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Downpatrick</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Báetán mac Cairill, Fiachnae mac Demmáin, Ruaidrí Mac Duinn Sléibe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Irish, later Middle Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dál Fiatach</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=814&amp;focus=dal-fiatach" aria-label="Dal Fiatach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4554 316 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dal Fiatach lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4874 572h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4874 572h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Downpatrick. <a href="/atlas?year=814&amp;focus=dal-fiatach">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

